  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO \nCOUNTIES...\n\nThe thin marine layer this morning allowed for only patchy clouds \nnear the coast and far western valleys. The strength of the high \npressure aloft is creating a larger inversion, where clouds may \nstick to the coast through the afternoon at times. This high has \npeaked near 600 dm, leading to the hottest temperatures of this heat \nwave. Temperatures were slightly increased for the afternoon based \non yesterday's heating. High temperatures will be well into the 90s \nfor coastal valleys, near 100-110 across the inland empire and San \nDiego foothills, and upper 80s and 90s in the mountain areas. The \nhigh pressure system begins to weaken on Sunday, allowing for a few \ndegrees of cooling. With the hot weather this weekend, heat risk \nwill be moderate to high, so please take proper precautions if going \noutdoors. Outdoor recreation is discouraged during the daytime!\n\nThe high weakens slowly, then stabilizes by Monday into the upcoming \nweek. This will bring temperatures near to 5 degrees above normal \nwith minimal day to day changes. Temperatures in the 90s to near 100 \nin the Inland Empire can be expected, with lower deserts seeing a \nslower cooldown with highs remaining over 110 degrees. Highs will be \nin the 80s for coastal valleys. Marine layer clouds will form each \nnight and morning within 15 miles of the coast next week.\n\nThe high pressure system will eventually move eastward by Wednesday \ninto the latter part of next week, bringing in greater monsoonal \nmoisture. PWAT's rise over an inch in the mountain and desert \nregions, but rainfall does not seem likely over the mountains by \nThursday and Friday with chances near 15% or less. Temperatures will \nstay relatively unchanged from the beginning of the week, with \nincreased humidity across the entire region.",
